---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Trim Paint vs Replace in Humid Florida | Repair Guide"
  description="Should you repair or replace trim in Florida? Humidity rot, peeling paint, and when replacement is worth it in Central Florida."
  ogImage="/images/crown-molding.jpg"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Article",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/trim-repair-vs-replacement-florida/#article",
        "headline": "Trim Repair vs Replacement in Humid Florida",
        "description": "Deciding whether to paint or replace trim in Florida's humid climate.",
        "author": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC."
        },
        "publisher": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC.",
          "logo": {
            "@type": "ImageObject",
            "url": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/images/logo.png"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/trim-repair-vs-replacement-florida/#breadcrumbs",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Home",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": "Guides",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": "Trim Repair vs Replacement",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/trim-repair-vs-replacement-florida"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }}
>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Trim Repair vs Replacement</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Trim Paint vs Replace in Humid Florida</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Should you repair or replace trim? Humidity rot, peeling paint, and cost comparison for Central Florida.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Florida humidity is hard on <a href="/services/trim-crown-molding" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">trim</a>. Baseboards near exterior doors, window trim, and crown molding can show peeling paint, soft spots, or rot after years of moisture exposure. Here's when to repair versus replace trim in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, and Central Florida homes.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">When to Repair & Repaint Trim</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most trim damage is cosmetic and fixable with proper prep and paint. Repair makes sense when:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Surface-level peeling:</strong> Paint peels but wood underneath is solid</li>
            <li><strong>Minor dings and nail holes:</strong> Small damage that fills and sands smooth</li>
            <li><strong>Caulk failure:</strong> Gaps between trim and wall, no wood damage</li>
            <li><strong>Cosmetic only:</strong> Trim looks dated but structurally sound</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Repair Process</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We scrape loose paint, sand smooth, fill holes and cracks with wood filler, prime bare wood, caulk gaps, and <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">paint</a> with semi-gloss or satin trim paint. Quality prep takes time but makes repainted trim last 5-10 years.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> Repainting interior trim costs $1-$3 per linear foot, depending on how much prep is needed. A typical room (60-80 linear feet of baseboards, door casings, window trim) costs $60-$240 to repair and repaint.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">When to Replace Trim</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Replace trim when damage goes beyond the paint layer. Signs you need replacement:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Soft or Rotten Wood</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If you press on trim with a screwdriver and it sinks in or feels spongy, the wood is rotting. Common spots: baseboards near exterior doors, window sills, trim near bathrooms and laundry rooms. Soft wood won't hold paint and gets worse over time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Warping or Bowing</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Trim that pulls away from the wall, bows out, or twists can't be straightened. This happens when moisture cycles (Florida humidity) cause wood to expand and contract repeatedly. Warped trim doesn't sit flush, creates visible gaps, and looks bad even after repainting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Multiple Layers of Old Paint</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If trim has 4-6+ layers of old paint, it loses crisp edges and details. Thick paint layers chip and peel more easily. Sometimes it's smarter to replace with fresh trim than strip and repaint heavily layered trim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">4. Damaged Beyond Repair</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Severe gouges, chunks missing, water damage that penetrates deep into the wood. If you'd need more wood filler than actual wood, replace it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Florida humidity test:</strong> Press a flat-head screwdriver gently into baseboards near exterior doors and bathroom thresholds. If it sinks in easily, moisture has compromised the wood—time to replace those sections.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Trim Replacement Cost</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Replacing trim costs more upfront but lasts 15-25 years if properly sealed and painted. Cost depends on trim style and material:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Baseboards</h3>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Paint-grade pine (3.5" or 5.5"):</strong> $2-$4/linear foot installed & painted</li>
            <li><strong>MDF baseboards (moisture-resistant):</strong> $2.50-$4.50/linear foot</li>
            <li><strong>Primed finger-joint pine:</strong> $3-$5/linear foot</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Crown Molding</h3>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Paint-grade pine (3.5" crown):</strong> $4-$7/linear foot installed & painted</li>
            <li><strong>MDF crown:</strong> $5-$8/linear foot</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            See our <a href="/guides/crown-molding-cost-kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">crown molding cost guide</a> for full pricing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Door & Window Casings</h3>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Paint-grade pine casing (2.5" or 3.5"):</strong> $3-$6/linear foot</li>
            <li><strong>Pre-primed MDF:</strong> $3.50-$6.50/linear foot</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">MDF vs. Wood Trim in Florida</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Both work, but each has trade-offs in Florida's humidity:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">MDF (Medium-Density Fiberboard)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Pros:</strong> Holds paint well, no knots, crisp edges, lower cost.<br/>
            <strong>Cons:</strong> Swells and disintegrates if it gets wet. Not recommended near exterior doors or moisture-prone areas (bathrooms, laundry).
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Solid Wood (Pine)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Pros:</strong> More moisture-resistant than MDF, repairable, traditional.<br/>
            <strong>Cons:</strong> Can have knots, costs slightly more, grain can telegraph through paint over time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Our Recommendation</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Use <strong>solid pine baseboards</strong> near exterior doors, bathrooms, and laundry rooms. Use <strong>MDF</strong> for interior crown molding, door casings, and baseboards in dry areas (bedrooms, living rooms). For high-moisture areas, some contractors use PVC or Azek trim (plastic)—more expensive but won't rot.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Decision Framework: Repair or Replace?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Use this quick decision tree:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Peeling paint, solid wood:</strong> Repair & repaint</li>
            <li><strong>Minor dings, no soft spots:</strong> Repair & repaint</li>
            <li><strong>Soft/spongy wood:</strong> Replace</li>
            <li><strong>Warped or bowed trim:</strong> Replace</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy paint layers (6+) or deep gouges:</strong> Replace</li>
            <li><strong>Cosmetic only, but doing full room repaint:</strong> Repair & repaint (cost-effective)</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Preventing Future Trim Damage</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Florida homes need trim maintenance to handle humidity:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Seal exposed edges:</strong> Caulk gaps between trim and walls, prime and paint all sides of trim (especially cut ends)</li>
            <li><strong>Use quality paint:</strong> Semi-gloss or satin trim paint holds up better in humidity than flat paint</li>
            <li><strong>Control interior moisture:</strong> Exhaust fans in bathrooms, fix leaks promptly, don't over-water indoor plants near trim</li>
            <li><strong>Inspect annually:</strong> Check baseboards near exterior doors and bathrooms for soft spots or peeling</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="text-center mt-12">
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-6 font-sans">Need trim repair or replacement in your Central Florida home?</p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    title="Trim Repair & Replacement in Central Florida"
    description="Proper trim work that lasts in Florida humidity. Serving Kissimmee, Orlando, and surrounding areas."
  />

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
